WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.886
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:09.080
JOANNA SMITH: Hi, I'm in
the dev zone once again,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.920
but now I'm with
Terrence Ryan, who's

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.800
one of my favorite co-workers.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.600
I'm not going to lie about that.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.630
But so this demo in particular,
it's a digital postcard.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:18.640
Is that right?

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.390
TERRY RYAN: Yeah,
that's kind of the idea.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.750
The idea is you take
a selfie or picture.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.080
And we will bounce it
around our network,

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.040
our private network
all around the world,

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.310
and print it out for you.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.282
So the pictures that
you get over here

00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:33.740
are pictures that have traveled
around the world digitally.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:34.782
JOANNA SMITH: Oh my gosh.

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:37.542
And so that's what these
stamps are, each data center.

00:00:37.542 --> 00:00:39.750
TERRY RYAN: Each data center,
we have a stamp for it.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.570
And it doesn't stamp it
until it's in that data set.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.190
JOANNA SMITH: Now when you
talk about a private network,

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.620
you mean the actual physical
cables and data centers

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.450
that Google built
around the world, right?

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.460
TERRY RYAN: Yeah, exactly,
the same data centers

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.760
and same infrastructure
that powers search and mail.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.970
Also under the covers, it's
available to Google Cloud

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.630
customers as their
private network.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.310
So whenever you talk between
one machine and another machine

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.590
on Google Cloud, it's always
traveling on our network.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.007
It never leaves the network.

00:01:05.007 --> 00:01:06.590
And so you get much
better performance

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.570
in terms of being
able to communicate

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.730
and security because
it's not leaving-- it's

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.020
not on the public internet.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:12.740
JOANNA SMITH: Oh, my gosh.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.090
This is actually one
of my favorite facts

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.020
about Google Cloud.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.300
It's a fun fact, which is
we built an infrastructure

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.395
to power our search.

00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:20.520
And then we started opening
that up to other people

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.710
so that you don't have
to reinvent the wheel.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.960
But it's really important
that we make it easy to use.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.302
Was this pretty easy to set up?

00:01:26.302 --> 00:01:27.260
TERRY RYAN: Absolutely.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.440
One of the great things
about Google Cloud,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.310
the default networking
in our projects,

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.102
is if all the machines are in
the same project, all the VMs--

00:01:34.102 --> 00:01:36.560
the VMs are what are powering
the relaying and the stamping

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.520
here--

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.777
if they're all in
the same project,

00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:42.110
then they are automatically
wired to talk to one another

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.940
without the developer
having to do any work

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.385
to make that happen.

00:01:45.385 --> 00:01:46.760
And so what that
allows you to do

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.790
is kind of get
started without having

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.883
to set up VPNs and VPCs
to get all that working.

00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:54.050
All of that is done for you.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.383
All the routing is done for you.

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.680
And if you want, this is
an open source project.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.650
So you can actually pull down,
and see how we set it up,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.100
and see that it really
is that super simple.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.030
JOANNA SMITH: And I like that it
makes it accessible to someone

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.080
who might not have
that networking

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.640
experience or
background, but so long

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.150
as they can
understand the theory,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.080
they can actually get going.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.120
TERRY RYAN: Yep, exactly.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.537
JOANNA SMITH: This
is so exciting.

00:02:12.537 --> 00:02:13.350
I love this demo.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.350
I am so happy to
take home my photos.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.690
And more importantly, the
fact that it is open source

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.455
means that anyone at
home can play along,

00:02:19.455 --> 00:02:21.830
even if you don't get to have
your own cute photo to take

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:22.362
home.

00:02:22.362 --> 00:02:23.820
And I hope y'all
have a great time.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.880
Thanks for joining
me in the Dev Zone.

